WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:05.100
When it comes to the villains of "The Handmaid's
Tale," Aunt Lydia is among the worst.

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:10.340
Charged with training and disciplining Handmaids,
Lydia has enraged and disturbed fans since

00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:12.139
the show's first season.

00:00:12.139 --> 00:00:16.059
This is her story, from her time as a teacher
to her future in Gilead.

00:00:17.210 --> 00:00:22.699
In season 3, we're introduced to a pre-Gilead
Lydia Clements, played by Ann Dowd.

00:00:22.699 --> 00:00:27.350
As it turns out, Aunt Lydia was once an elementary
school teacher with a background in family

00:00:27.350 --> 00:00:28.350
law.

00:00:28.350 --> 00:00:31.460
This Lydia wore her hair down, and was once
married.

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:36.110
Even in this era, however, Lydia was pretty
judgmental, especially when it came to religion

00:00:36.110 --> 00:00:37.620
and parenting.

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:42.379
This is explored in flashbacks, which see
Lydia befriend a young single mother named

00:00:42.379 --> 00:00:44.230
Noelle and her son, Ryan.

00:00:44.230 --> 00:00:47.710
The trio grow close, and even spend Christmas
together.

00:00:47.710 --> 00:00:51.079
Lydia seems to revel in being called "Aunt
Lydia" by Ryan.

00:00:51.079 --> 00:00:55.400
However, she frowns on what she sees as some
poor parenting choices by Noelle.

00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:58.140
Speaking to “Vanity Fair” after the episode,
Dowd said,

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:01.600
"I think she, at that point in her life, really
wants to help.

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:06.330
I think she probably got out of [family law]
because it was too painful to see the separation,

00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:07.330
to see the loss."

00:01:07.330 --> 00:01:12.080
The only other time Lydia alluded to her past
was during Season 2's "Smart Power," after

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:14.659
June asks her to protect her unborn baby.

00:01:14.659 --> 00:01:21.310
"I was godmother to my sister's child.

00:01:21.310 --> 00:01:23.060
He died when he was four days old."

00:01:24.490 --> 00:01:29.740
Soon after Noelle gives Lydia a makeup set
for Christmas, Lydia goes on a date with Jim,

00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:31.760
the principal of the school where she works.

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:35.530
But after they begin kissing, Jim suddenly
wants to stop.

00:01:35.530 --> 00:01:38.100
While he likes her, Jim thinks they're rushing
things.

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:42.250
Instead of understanding, Lydia flies into
a rage, then catches a glimpse of herself

00:01:42.250 --> 00:01:44.409
with make-up running down her face.

00:01:44.409 --> 00:01:46.530
Lydia's emotions here are complex.

00:01:46.530 --> 00:01:48.510
As Ann Dowd explained to “Vanity Fair,”

00:01:48.510 --> 00:01:53.440
"I was raised in a strong Catholic home, you
know — sex is a very, very tricky area.

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:56.280
And there's a lot of guilt and a lot of shame
associated with it.

00:01:56.280 --> 00:02:00.659
I think what I loved about that moment is
it said so much about how she was raised,

00:02:00.659 --> 00:02:03.740
and that, honestly, shame is in charge of
her life."

00:02:03.740 --> 00:02:06.240
Lydia's shame quickly turns to revenge.

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:09.789
After blaming Noelle for convincing her to
go on the date, she does everything in her

00:02:09.789 --> 00:02:12.400
power to separate Noelle from her son.

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:16.950
After Noelle angrily confronts Lydia, Jim
walks off in disgust after seeing Lydia's

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:18.140
true colors.

00:02:18.140 --> 00:02:22.200
She justifies her actions by telling herself
that Ryan is better off without Noelle.

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:27.860
Aunt Lydia uses fear, abuse, and religious
manipulation to train Handmaids to give up

00:02:27.860 --> 00:02:30.650
their bodily autonomy for the sake of Gilead.

00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:35.780
According to Lydia, the Handmaids' horrific
lives are divinely ordained, and thus beyond

00:02:35.780 --> 00:02:37.030
human doubt.

00:02:37.030 --> 00:02:42.150
Flashbacks to the Red Center see Lydia training
one of the first groups of Handmaids, including

00:02:42.150 --> 00:02:43.940
June, Moira, and Janine.

00:02:43.940 --> 00:02:47.780
She is immediately set up as a villain who
uses torture to get the Handmaids to comply.

00:02:47.780 --> 00:02:51.930
For example, she doesn't think twice about
removing Janine's eye when Janine resists

00:02:51.930 --> 00:02:53.540
becoming a Handmaid.

00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:58.380
After the Handmaids are placed in homes, Lydia
also serves as a liaison between the government

00:02:58.380 --> 00:03:00.980
of Gilead and individual households.

00:03:00.980 --> 00:03:05.130
She frequently makes house calls to ensure
that everyone is following the rules.

00:03:05.130 --> 00:03:09.260
In the first season, we see her make a handful
of visits to the Waterford residence, where

00:03:09.260 --> 00:03:11.050
June has been placed.

00:03:11.050 --> 00:03:15.380
Lydia frequently asserts her power over June,
and at one point even beats her with a cattle

00:03:15.380 --> 00:03:19.290
prod before the Commander's wife, Serena Joy,
stops her.

00:03:20.290 --> 00:03:24.310
Lydia is responsible for devising many of
Gilead's abusive "training" techniques, which

00:03:24.310 --> 00:03:29.310
include ordering Handmaids to hold rocks for
hours in the rain, getting them to kill "traitors"

00:03:29.310 --> 00:03:33.050
with their bare hands, and making them believe
they're about to be hanged.

00:03:33.050 --> 00:03:37.209
She also orchestrates most of the physical
mutilation that occurs, including removing

00:03:37.209 --> 00:03:41.159
Janine's eye and performing female circumcision
on Emily.

00:03:41.159 --> 00:03:46.360
When she isn't violently disciplining Handmaids,
Lydia is the ultimate gaslighter.

00:03:46.360 --> 00:03:51.360
She twists women psychologically in a variety
of ways, including victim-blaming and setting

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:53.569
the Handmaids against each other.

00:03:53.569 --> 00:03:57.720
Lydia is particularly abusive towards June,
and is quick to shame her into believing that

00:03:57.720 --> 00:04:00.810
her choices are what bring harm and death
to others.

00:04:00.810 --> 00:04:06.080
It's all done in a bid to keep June compliant,
which works — but only for a while.

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:09.049
Lydia refers to the Handmaids as her "girls."

00:04:09.049 --> 00:04:13.709
In fact, Lydia often paints herself as the
Handmaids' protector, and is quick to blame

00:04:13.709 --> 00:04:15.330
herself when they fail.

00:04:15.330 --> 00:04:19.130
In her own twisted way, she believes she really
does care about them.

00:04:19.130 --> 00:04:20.959
As Dowd explained in 2017,

00:04:20.959 --> 00:04:26.000
"At the core of her choices, she loves these
girls deeply and wants them to succeed in

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:30.690
this new world, and she is keenly aware that
if she doesn't get them to get the drill immediately,

00:04:30.690 --> 00:04:32.530
they are not going to make it."

00:04:32.530 --> 00:04:37.410
This twisted motherliness is apparent when
Serena tells Aunt Lydia to remove Janine from

00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:39.889
an important dinner because of her missing
eye.

00:04:39.889 --> 00:04:42.139
Then, Lydia seems to sympathize with Janine.

00:04:42.139 --> 00:04:46.820
But later, she proves her loyalty to Gilead
by ordering the other Handmaids to stone Janine

00:04:46.820 --> 00:04:47.820
to death.

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:49.960
The Handmaids, led by June, refuse.

00:04:49.960 --> 00:04:54.310
Rather than let the guards shoot them for
their disobedience, Lydia asks them to spare

00:04:54.310 --> 00:04:55.510
the Handmaids.

00:04:55.510 --> 00:04:58.020
"These girls are my responsibility."

00:04:58.020 --> 00:05:03.441
In Season 2, it is revealed that Lydia sent
Janine to the Colonies, and punished the Handmaids

00:05:03.441 --> 00:05:05.490
with a mock execution.

00:05:06.490 --> 00:05:08.760
Emily suffers greatly at Lydia's hands.

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:13.250
After discovering that Emily is in a romantic
relationship with a Martha, Lydia has Emily's

00:05:13.250 --> 00:05:16.160
lover murdered and Emily mutilated.

00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:20.850
When Emily seeks revenge by killing a guardian,
Lydia has her sent to the Colonies.

00:05:20.850 --> 00:05:24.820
She eventually brings Emily back, because
they're short on Handmaids, and pairs her

00:05:24.820 --> 00:05:26.180
with Commander Lawrence.

00:05:26.180 --> 00:05:30.100
Finally, in the Season 2 finale, Emily stabs
Lydia in the back.

00:05:30.100 --> 00:05:35.110
While Lydia survives, the event changes her,
making her an even harder character.

00:05:35.110 --> 00:05:39.630
Show creator Bruce Miller explained the impact
Emily had on Lydia, telling “Forbes,”

00:05:39.630 --> 00:05:42.229
"She thinks there's love between her and her
girls.

00:05:42.229 --> 00:05:45.870
The fact that one of her girls has literally
stabbed her in the back, I think that alters

00:05:45.870 --> 00:05:50.949
your workplace feelings on a day to day basis
[...] I think in her case, it makes her double-down.

00:05:50.949 --> 00:05:53.820
She feels like she just wasn't strong enough
in her discipline.

00:05:53.820 --> 00:05:57.039
So, she has decided it's time to get tough."

00:05:57.039 --> 00:06:01.250
After Emily's escape from Gilead at the end
of Season 2, Lydia can't take her feelings

00:06:01.250 --> 00:06:02.410
out on her.

00:06:02.410 --> 00:06:06.210
Instead, Lydia comes down even harder on the
remaining Handmaids.

00:06:06.210 --> 00:06:10.210
Eventually, she snaps after Janine begs to
return to her former family and her infant

00:06:10.210 --> 00:06:11.210
daughter.

00:06:11.210 --> 00:06:15.930
Lydia beats her mercilessly in front of a
crowd, only stopping when June comes between

00:06:15.930 --> 00:06:16.930
them.

00:06:17.930 --> 00:06:20.680
The relationship between Lydia and June is
rough from the start.

00:06:20.680 --> 00:06:25.389
After an antagonistic first season, they seem
to grow closer in Season 2 as a pregnant June

00:06:25.389 --> 00:06:29.330
leans into Lydia, seeking protection for her
unborn baby.

00:06:29.330 --> 00:06:33.150
During this time period, we learn that Lydia
has some power over the Commanders and their

00:06:33.150 --> 00:06:38.190
wives as well, since she is tasked with evaluating
whether the household is a good fit for a

00:06:38.190 --> 00:06:39.190
child.

00:06:39.190 --> 00:06:42.039
The short-lived peace between June and Lydia
doesn't last, however.

00:06:42.039 --> 00:06:46.540
When June orchestrates her daughter Nichole's
escape, Lydia comes down hard on her.

00:06:46.540 --> 00:06:51.000
She forces June to participate in propaganda
videos with the Waterfords, and even accompanies

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:53.230
the family on a trip to Washington.

00:06:53.230 --> 00:06:58.230
While there, she seems disturbed by the sight
of Handmaids whose mouths have been sewn shut.

00:06:58.230 --> 00:07:01.930
Lydia is very much aware that June's biggest
weakness is her daughter, Hannah, who was

00:07:01.930 --> 00:07:05.200
kidnapped by Gilead when June's family tried
to escape.

00:07:05.200 --> 00:07:09.580
In Season 3, after June tracks down Hannah,
Lydia orders the execution of the Martha who

00:07:09.580 --> 00:07:11.940
helped June connect with her daughter.

00:07:11.940 --> 00:07:16.190
As if that wasn't enough, Lydia forces June
and the other Handmaids to be the ones to

00:07:16.190 --> 00:07:17.190
execute her.

00:07:18.190 --> 00:07:23.180
After June helps a planeload of Gilead children
flee to Canada in the Season 3 finale, Lydia

00:07:23.180 --> 00:07:24.410
comes under fire.

00:07:24.410 --> 00:07:29.400
As a result, Lydia makes it her mission in
Season 4 to find and reform June.

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:30.449
According to Miller,

00:07:30.449 --> 00:07:34.800
"Lydia in this season [is in a] position where
she is just obsessed with June and has so

00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:37.789
much of her personal worth tied up in what
June is doing."

00:07:37.789 --> 00:07:42.169
As the season progresses, Lydia and June continue
to circle one another, although Lydia never

00:07:42.169 --> 00:07:45.360
manages to keep June within her clutches for
long.

00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:50.240
But even when they're apart, they each continue
to occupy a significant portion of the other's

00:07:50.240 --> 00:07:51.240
thoughts.

00:07:51.240 --> 00:07:52.240
Miller explained,

00:07:52.240 --> 00:07:56.810
"Although [June and Lydia] are kind of enemies
in this story, they are inextricably linked.

00:07:56.810 --> 00:08:00.699
They spend a ton of time thinking about the
other one and what the other one is doing.

00:08:00.699 --> 00:08:04.940
So it's fascinating to have two people who
really would like to be both rid of each other

00:08:04.940 --> 00:08:07.360
but who are tangled up with each other all
the time."

00:08:08.360 --> 00:08:13.770
After June escapes yet again in Season 4,
Aunt Lydia is no longer in Gilead's good graces.

00:08:13.770 --> 00:08:17.270
For the first time, we see Lydia's sparse
living quarters.

00:08:17.270 --> 00:08:20.750
After being stripped of her responsibilities,
she's expected to spend the rest of her days

00:08:20.750 --> 00:08:23.770
doing puzzles and playing cards with her fellow
Aunts.

00:08:23.770 --> 00:08:28.300
However, Lydia wants her power back and is
not interested in being forced into an early

00:08:28.300 --> 00:08:29.300
retirement.

00:08:29.300 --> 00:08:33.779
So, Lydia strikes a deal with Commander Lawrence:
She'll give him dirt on his fellow Commanders

00:08:33.779 --> 00:08:36.060
if he'll restore her to her former station.

00:08:36.060 --> 00:08:40.640
She also wants to be the one to punish June
if and when she is finally caught.

00:08:41.640 --> 00:08:45.820
After Lydia regains her power, it's evident
that she is still haunted by her failure with

00:08:45.820 --> 00:08:46.820
June.

00:08:46.820 --> 00:08:50.670
After learning that June escaped to Canada,
she quickly spirals out of control.

00:08:50.670 --> 00:08:54.730
In Episode 8, she takes out her frustrations
on one of her charges, and then even turns

00:08:54.730 --> 00:08:55.730
on a fellow Aunt.

00:08:55.730 --> 00:08:57.560
Speaking to “Elle,” Dowd said,

00:08:57.560 --> 00:08:59.390
"She holds herself responsible.

00:08:59.390 --> 00:09:00.700
So do the commanders.

00:09:00.700 --> 00:09:02.070
She knew something was happening.

00:09:02.070 --> 00:09:03.070
She knew it.

00:09:03.070 --> 00:09:05.030
And she did not follow it to its core.

00:09:05.030 --> 00:09:09.700
And June, whom she has a tremendously complicated
relationship with, pulled it off.

00:09:09.700 --> 00:09:13.060
So there's that personal factor where she's
being tough on herself because she knows all

00:09:13.060 --> 00:09:17.500
of that, and also desperate to prove that,
'No, no, no, I'm not irrelevant.'"

00:09:18.529 --> 00:09:23.420
After losing June, Lydia gets a second chance
to reform Janine after Janine is recaptured

00:09:23.420 --> 00:09:24.800
in Chicago.

00:09:24.800 --> 00:09:29.170
Following Lydia's outburst, Commander Lawrence
tells her to take her anger out on Janine,

00:09:29.170 --> 00:09:31.500
whose life is now considered expendable.

00:09:31.500 --> 00:09:36.029
In a rare moment of weakness, Lydia reunites
with Janine and actually heeds her request

00:09:36.029 --> 00:09:38.130
not to be assigned to another Commander.

00:09:38.130 --> 00:09:41.190
Instead Lydia decides to keep Janine at the
center.

00:09:41.190 --> 00:09:45.340
At first, the other Aunts question why Lydia
isn't doing God's work and putting Janine

00:09:45.340 --> 00:09:49.380
back into the field, but Lydia finds a use
for her before long.

00:09:49.380 --> 00:09:54.200
When the former Mrs. Keyes reappears as the
handmaid Esther, the traumatized girl mounts

00:09:54.200 --> 00:09:57.399
a hunger strike that none of the Aunts can
stop.

00:09:57.399 --> 00:10:02.380
Aunt Lydia agrees to let Janine talk to her,
and Janine is able to convince Esther to comply,

00:10:02.380 --> 00:10:06.750
warning her that if she doesn't, the consequences
will be worse than death.

00:10:06.750 --> 00:10:10.920
Janine's assistance earns Aunt Lydia's trust
and helps reestablish Lydia's power among

00:10:10.920 --> 00:10:11.920
the other Aunts.

00:10:12.920 --> 00:10:17.440
While Lydia's future within the world of Hulu's
"The Handmaid's Tale" remains unwritten, Margaret

00:10:17.440 --> 00:10:22.269
Atwood took matters into her own hands with
her 2019 novel, "The Testaments."

00:10:22.269 --> 00:10:26.980
A sequel to her original novel, "The Testaments"
is written from three perspectives, including

00:10:26.980 --> 00:10:27.980
Aunt Lydia's.

00:10:27.980 --> 00:10:31.760
It takes place 15 years after the events of
the first novel, which were covered in the

00:10:31.760 --> 00:10:33.740
first season of the show.

00:10:33.740 --> 00:10:37.960
In this sequel, Atwood reveals that, in the
early days of Gilead, Lydia was rounded up

00:10:37.960 --> 00:10:39.320
with many other women.

00:10:39.320 --> 00:10:45.260
She was detained, tortured, and eventually
given a choice: Cooperate or face the consequences.

00:10:45.260 --> 00:10:49.220
Lydia sees herself as someone who did what
she had to do in order to survive.

00:10:49.220 --> 00:10:52.880
Eventually, in the book, she becomes integral
to the fall of Gilead.

00:10:52.880 --> 00:10:57.190
How much this novel will affect the future
of the show remains to be seen.

00:10:57.190 --> 00:11:01.290
Atwood's Lydia is a very different character
in many respects: in the book, Lydia was a

00:11:01.290 --> 00:11:06.220
judge prior to Gilead, and acts as a mole
for the rebellious organization Mayday.

00:11:06.220 --> 00:11:11.410
For now, one thing is sure: Aunt Lydia will
definitely go down as one of the most compelling

00:11:11.410 --> 00:11:13.730
TV villains of all time.

